                               SIMULATION MODE
      
         The biggest new feature in Strategic Falcon version 1.1 is 
         almost invisible, yet has a tremendous impact upon how you wage 
         war. It's called "Simulation Mode" ("Sim Mode"). 
         
         Introduction:
         
         The purpose of Strategic Falcon has been, since the outset, to 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update                Page 4
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         add a dimension of strategy to Falcon 3.0. That's where its name 
         came from. Up to this point, however, the strategic elements of 
         game play have been vague and elusive. For example, it's been 
         hard to see any direct correlation between persistent strikes on 
         a specific site and future capture of that site (although this 
         correlation does indeed exist!). These strategic elements have 
         remained elusive for one major reason: there have been no 
         features in StratFalc that would override any of Falcon's own 
         strategic theater decisions. It's one thing to allow you to 
         place units that would weaken an enemy site and, after the 
         attack, let Falcon 3.0 decide whether that site would become 
         yours; it's a whole different animal to let StratFalc make the 
         decision and override Falcon.
         
         With its new Simulation Mode, StratFalc has taken the plunge, 
         and done so in a rather large way. While leaving Falcon's 
         "mission to mission" functions totally alone, and still allowing 
         it to carry out its own strategic theater operations (that is, 
         deciding at 0600 every day what is yours and what is the 
         enemy's), you now have a method to strategically capture a site.
         
         That's not all. Allied aircraft are now grouped into their own 
         squadrons and assigned to specific bases, and any ground units 
         you use for site attacks must come from one of the three 
         brigades that you are assigned at the outset of a campaign.
         
         Bear in mind that you do not have to use Sim Mode at all. You 
         can go back to the previous mode of operation simply by checking 
         the "Sim Mode--Off" box in the Other/Config submode. Don't 
         forget to press "Save Configuration" if you want it to stay off 
         for future StratFalc sessions. 
         
         Everything detailed in this section only occurs if you have Sim 
         Mode enabled.
         
         When you start a new campaign, StratFalc will calculate the 
         quantity of all aircraft and ground units that you have at your 
         disposal to prosecute your various attacks. It bases this total 
         on how many units are at each of your sites, but also considers 
         the appropriate Order of Battle given in the Falcon 
         documentation, as well as the difficulty level currently set in 
         Falcon 3.0 itself. 
         
         
         Aircraft:
         
                              Aircraft: Overview
         
         Based on its calculations, Strategic Falcon generates specific 
         quantities of each aircraft type. This quantity is all you get 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update                Page 5
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         for the entire campaign; unlike Falcon 3.0, there are no 
         *direct* replacements at any time! Use them wisely!
         
         StratFalc assigns all aircraft types to their own squadron and 
         each squadron to its own base. In other words, if the order of 
         battle includes a squadron of 12 F-15s, 8 B-52s, 10 A-10s, and 8 
         F-111s (the list would continue--you receive some of each type 
         of available aircraft), and the allies currently hold three 
         active airbases--call them, in order of distance from the enemy, 
         Airbase A, Airbase B, and Airbase C--then StratFalc might place 
         the F-15s and A-10s at A, the F-111s at B, and the B-52s at C.
         (Thanks to Chuck Corway, Dom Killers, and the others in 
         FSFORUM who helped with the appropriate squadron names.)
         
         When you add a flight of friendly aircraft to a mission, 
         StratFalc will automatically place its starting waypoint 
         (waypoint 0) at the appropriate base, and will place waypoint 
         1 in the current center of the map. (You will be free to move 
         waypoint 0, but wouldn't do so if you were playing 
         realistically.)
         
         You can transfer the squadrons between airbases after this 
         initial setup, but doing so will take anywhere from two to 
         twenty-four hours (depending on distance between the two bases 
         involved, how busy they are, and the state of your campaign), 
         and the aircraft in that squadron will be unavailable during the 
         move.
         
         After you fly the mission and return to Strategic Falcon at the 
         beginning of the next one, StratFalc calculates the losses of 
         any aircraft that were placed "at risk" in the previous 
         mission. THE LOSSES DEPEND DIRECTLY UPON YOUR SUCCESS IN 
         ACCOMPLISHING *YOUR* MISSION, as well as the current campaign 
         difficulty setting in Falcon3. In other words, if you succeed 
         in your primary goal, aircraft losses will be minimal or nil. 
         If you fail, you'll lose some or all of the aircraft that you 
         sent out on missions. If you lose aircraft, StratFalc will 
         tell you so; if you don't see any such announcement, you can 
         safely assume all your StratFalc aircraft survived.
         
         There are also many degrees of success. While accomplishing the 
         primary goal is the main criterion, StratFalc also tracks how 
         many of your F-16s were destroyed during the mission, how many 
         enemy aircraft were shot down and their types, and how many 
         ground units and installations were destroyed and their type. 
         Generally speaking, though, if you succeed in your primary 
         mission and lose none of your own (F-16 slot) planes, you'll 
         lose none of the StratFalc planes, either.
         
         (The losses are calculated in this manner because there is no 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update                Page 6
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         way available to directly collect the Falcon3 "debrief" info 
         from each mission.)
         
                         Aircraft: Controls Available
         
         All controls for the Sim Mode are grouped in the STRAT mode 
         under the SIM submode (it used to be "Attk"). You'll see there 
         are now four radio buttons at the bottom of the control panel: 
         "Attk," "Grnd," "Air," and "Bases." (If Sim Mode is turned off, 
         the latter 3 radio buttons will be grayed out.)
         
         For air operations, the two controls that concern us are the 
         "Air" and "Bases" buttons. 
         
         If you select "Air," the squadron controls fill the top part of 
         the panel. You'll notice that the map returns to zoom factor one 
         and 0,0 origin, in order to give you a view of the entire 
         theater. Any airbases that you occupy will show up in yellow.
         
         Using the "" button, you can cycle through all your 
         squadrons and discover their names, aircraft type, quantity, 
         and current base. On the map, an icon will appear at the 
         appropriate place. 
         
         You can also cycle through the squadrons at any base by 
         right-clicking the mouse on that base. You can jump to a base 
         by left-clicking directly on it. If the base has no 
         squadrons, you'll hear a beep.
         
         To transfer a squadron, simply grab its icon with the left mouse 
         button and drag the mouse to the base desired. (You'll only be 
         able to transfer squadrons to those sites shown in yellow, 
         with the exception of the carrier, which you won't be able 
         to transfer to at all.) A line will extend from the squadron 
         icon to the new base.
         
         You'll be informed how long the transfer will take and asked to 
         confirm the move. If you do confirm it, a number will appear 
         below the base name. This number indicates how many hours 
         remain until the squadron transfer is completed. A blue "D" 
         will appear at the destination base for any squadrons being 
         transferred. Once you give the order to transfer a squadron, 
         the squadron will be unavailable until it reaches its new 
         base. You won't be able to change its destination once the 
         order has been confirmed.
         
         There's also an aircraft carrier involved; you'll see it as a 
         yellow square off the shore in those theaters that have water 
         (that's all but Pakistan). Your F-14, F/A-18, and F-4 
         squadrons will be based on the carrier. You'll be able to 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update                Page 7
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         transfer squadrons off the carrier, but never *to* it. (And 
         no, you *can't* move the carrier!) 
      
         At the bottom of the "Air" screen is a "Summary" button. This 
         gives you an overall view of your air force. For each 
         squadron it will tell you the aircraft type, quantity, how 
         many are already committed to missions, the name of the base, 
         and the squadron name.
         
         If you select "Bases" from the Strat/Sim submode, you get 
         information about each airbase. This information includes 
         which squadrons are based there: the type of plane and how 
         many of that type are available.
         
         Also included is an "Evacuate!" button. If you're afraid the 
         enemy may be about to take over one of your bases, it's a good 
         idea to evacuate it. More information is given in the "Details" 
         section below.
         
                              Aircraft: Details
         
         -Mixed Aircraft Types-
         
         Use of squadrons is largely automatic. If you add a flight of 
         aircraft, its starting waypoint will automatically be placed 
         on the proper base.
         
         A wrinkle appears when you mix aircraft types in the same 
         flight. What StratFalc does in this case is assign the flight to 
         the airbase of the lead plane. For instance, if you have a 
         flight of two A-10s led by an F-15, the flight will originate at 
         the F-15 squadron's base, not the A-10s'. It is assumed that the 
         A-10s have flown to the leader's base before the mission begins. 
         Note, however, that the A-10s are not "free"--they still come 
         from the A-10 squadron, wherever it is!
         
         Another twist is F-16 flight slots. If you assign another 
         aircraft type to be the leader of an F-16 flight (meaning your 
         wingmen are flying other types of aircraft), StratFalc will ask 
         you if you want to move that flight to the proper base. What 
         this means is your pilots are temporarily flying out of that 
         base. Again, you can override this setup and move the starting 
         waypoint wherever you want it.
         
         What happens when you change a lead aircraft type AFTER a flight 
         has already been laid out? Well, StratFalc asks you what you 
         want to do. You can either leave the flight like it is or have 
         StratFalc move its first waypoint to the proper base.

         -Evacuation-
         If the enemy is closing in on one of your bases and you want to




      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update                Page 8
      --------------------------------------------------------------------


         get all your planes out, you should hit the "Evacuate!" button.
         If you do this, all squadrons at that base become unavailable
         and they scatter to any other available bases. You have no say
         over where they're going.

         You may wonder why this would be necessary--why not just
         transfer the squadrons manually? Because there's no guarantee
         that a manual transfer order will be carried out within the next
         six hours. A transfer can sometimes take a full day to complete
         (and here StratFalc is being easy on you!). An evacuation is
         basically a scramble: everyone fuels up and flies, with whatever
         JP-5 they can get in their plane and whatever stores they can
         get on its wings.

         If a base is captured while squadrons are still there, you WILL
         lose those squadrons!  An exception:  any squadrons with units
         currently committed will not evacuate.
         
         -Aircraft Placed by Falcon-
         
         When you begin a new mission, StratFalc examines the flights 
         that are generated by Falcon. If it determines that any of these 
         flights is an escort or support flight for your F-16s, it will 
         ask if you want to move that flight to its proper base. 
         
         If you answer "No," the starting waypoints of these (which 
         normally start very close to your airbase) will remain where 
         they are.
         
         If StratFalc determines that the flight in question is directly 
         related to your mission (B-52s to be escorted, for instance), 
         it will leave them alone (it is assumed that they've flown to 
         their starting waypoints and are just reaching your base when 
         the mission starts). StratFalc does this to avoid interfering 
         with Falcon's mission-generating logic--especially when an 
         escort mission is involved. However, these aircraft still come 
         out of your squadrons! For instance, if Falcon gives you four
         B-52s, those Buffs come from your B-52 squadron, and if you lose
         the mission, you'll (probably) also lose those B-52s.
         
         There's a flip side to this. It is also the only way to get 
         aircraft "replacements" in Strategic Falcon. Say your F-15 
         squadron is wiped out in the course of the war. If, in a future 
         mission, Falcon allots a flight of 2 F-15s to you, these F-15s 
         become your replacements, as long as you don't change their 
         aircraft type to something else before flying the mission (in 
         other words, keep the F-15s as F-15s, don't make them A-10s or 
         something). If you succeed in this mission and all the F-15s 
         don't get destroyed, then the surviving F-15s become your newly 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update                Page 9
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         revived F-15 squadron.
         
         Note that this only applies to Falcon-supplied aircraft when 
         your own squadron of that type is either destroyed or doesn't 
         have enough of that aircraft type to satisfy Falcon. In the 
         above example, if you had 2 F-15s in your F-15 squadron, the 
         Falcon aircraft are taken from there. If you had only one F-15, 
         then you have a chance to add a second one.
         
         -The Enemy Squadrons-
         
         With all this tracking of aircraft, you might wonder: what about 
         the enemy squadrons? Is anybody keeping track of those? I could 
         lie to you and say yes (you might even believe me!). But no, the 
         enemy aircraft aren't tracked--and there's a reason why 
         StratFalc doesn't bother: keeping tabs on who's been destroyed 
         implies that StratFalc would do something about it. 
         
         In other words, if StratFalc decided at the beginning of a 
         campaign that the enemy had 16 MiG-29s and kept count of them, 
         then when you had destroyed all those MiG-29s you'd expect to 
         see no more (logically enough). For StratFalc to do that, 
         however, it would have to step in and directly alter any future 
         Falcon-generated mission that used MiG-29s. Other than moving 
         the starting waypoints of some flights as discussed above, 
         Strategic Falcon will never directly alter any Falcon mission 
         (it leaves that up to you!). To do so is just asking for 
         trouble, as well as angry calls to technical support.
         
         If this bothers you, just keep tabs on the enemy yourself 
         and, when appropriate, change the aircraft types accordingly. 
         Falcon does seem to track them itself to some degree; the 
         enemy aircraft seem to get "easier" as a campaign goes on.
         
      
         Ground Units:
         
                            Ground Units: Overview
         
         In addition to calculating the available air units, StratFalc 
         will track your ground units. At the beginning of the campaign, 
         SF tallies up all the ground units at all sites you occupy. 
         Again depending upon the Falcon3 campaign difficulty setting, it 
         adds an additional quantity of units that become the brigades of 
         the "1st Armored Division." These are all the units that you 
         have available to carry out ground attacks. 
         
         The 1st Armored Division begins the campaign at your home air 
         base. For the purposes of Strategic Falcon, the battalions of 
         the Division are grouped into three brigades--two armor and one 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 10
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         mechanized infantry. The brigades are stand-alone units inside 
         the 1st Armored Division and so move independently.
         
         Although the smallest unit that you can move is the brigade 
         itself, the brigades are each (internally) further broken down 
         into four battalions. An armored brigade will have two armored 
         battalions, one mech infantry battalion, and one artillery 
         battalion. A mechanized infantry battalion is the same, but one 
         of the armored battalions is replaced with a second mech 
         infantry battalion.             
         
         The movement of brigades is fully discussed in "Details" below, 
         but to give you an overview of the way they move: missions in 
         Falcon occur at six hour intervals. It just so happens that 
         "turns," for brigade movement purposes, are also based on a six 
         hour interval. To keep a close eye on your brigades, therefore, 
         it's best to come back into StratFalc after every Falcon 
         mission. 
         
         If you want to launch a ground strike against an enemy site, 
         the site must be within 15 miles of one of your brigades. 
         Each time you place a ground path--commit to a 
         strike--StratFalc will ask you which brigade you want to use. 
         You can place up to sixty units in up to four ground paths, 
         depending on what else is going on in the current mission. 
         These units are placed "at risk."
         
         If you use the "Attk" screen to place the units, StratFalc will
         only allow you to attack sites that are within 15 miles of a 
         brigade; if more than one brigade is within that distance, 
         StratFalc will take the units from the closest one.
         
         Just as with "at risk" aircraft, after you fly the current 
         mission and return to Strategic Falcon at the beginning of the 
         next one, StratFalc calculates the losses of your ground units. 
         Again, the losses depend directly upon your success in 
         accomplishing *your* mission, as well as the other factors 
         discussed in the "aircraft" section above.  StratFalc will 
         report any losses.
         
         To commit to an all out assault in an attempt to capture a site, 
         you simply make the site the destination of one of your 
         brigades. StratFalc examines the situation and calculates the 
         outcome of the battle. If you are victorious, you will occupy 
         the site.
         
         What about the enemy brigades? They're there, but you'll won't 
         see them until it's too late! The only time you'll discover 
         their presence is when you run into them while you're behind 
         enemy lines--and you'll take heavy losses if you do! The enemy 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 11
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         tends to keep his brigades entrenched in his own territory, and 
         he normally only uses them for reinforcement of his strategic 
         sites. They'll rarely cross over into your territory. 
         
         However, if you stray too deeply into the red zone, you can very 
         easily find yourself facing his units. They'll be well dug in 
         and they won't even start shooting until your flank passes their 
         forward elements. Entire brigades have been known to be wiped 
         out before they have a chance to extricate! The lesson here: 
         don't go beyond the forward edge of the battle area unless you 
         need to! And if you need to, make the foray as short as 
         possible--your chances of running into the enemy increase with 
         each mission that you remain behind the lines. (As a rule, 
         attack the sites on the front line and capture them--only 
         advance as you push the enemy back. Although it is often *very* 
         tempting to make an end run for one or two sites that you 
         need to meet victory conditions, if you get bogged down back 
         there, you'll be ambushed and very likely will lose your 
         brigade.)
         
         If you do get entangled with an enemy brigade, the *only* way 
         to get away from it is to get your brigade back into friendly 
         territory. Unfortunately, your units will move very slowly 
         once they're engaged.
      
                       Ground Units: Controls Available
         
         The controls available to assist your command of the ground 
         forces are found in the "Grnd" screen in the "Strat/Sim" 
         submode. These include radio buttons to select the brigade to 
         which you wish to issue orders, radio buttons to set the 
         brigade's speed, a button to bring up the "Order of Battle" 
         box (this box details the current strength of the brigade), 
         and a box which informs you if the brigade is currently in 
         enemy territory.
         
         If a brigade has been destroyed, its name next to the radio 
         buttons will be grayed out and will be unselectable. If all of 
         your brigades are wiped out, StratFalc won't let you into the 
         "Grnd" screen at all. (You can always turn off "Sim Mode" in 
         configuration if you want to place a ground attack but have lost 
         all your brigades.)
         
         To move a brigade, either select the radio button of the one you 
         want or click on its icon on the map. Next, "drag" it to its 
         destination. A line will extend from the brigade to the point 
         you indicated.
         
         To reset the destination, click inside the small box at the end 
         of the destination line and move it to the new location. To 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 12
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         clear any motion of the brigade, click on the brigade's icon 
         itself.
         
         The circle around the brigade shows its striking 
         distance--it's the fifteen mile radius inside of which you 
         can carry out "Attrition Attacks" (explained below).
         
         To set the brigade's speed, click on the appropriate radio 
         button. Please read the "Details" section below for some 
         important information on how brigades move.
         
       
                            Ground Units: Details
         
         -Capturing Enemy Sites-
         
         One thing has to be made clear: with the addition of Sim Mode, 
         you now have two kinds of attacks at your disposal. First, you 
         have the old type, where you place ground units at an enemy site 
         and they go in and blast it out with the bad guys. This is 
         called an "Attrition Attack" and is used to weaken an enemy site 
         in support of future or current air operations, as well as to 
         put the odds in your favor for future capture of the site.
         
         To carry out an Attrition Attack, just do what you've always 
         done in StratFalc: you either use the "Attk" screen in the 
         Strat/Sim submode or you place units manually using the 
         Tact/Alld Ground submode. This is fully explained in the 
         Strategic Falcon manual. Remember to use one of the site 
         "activation" methods! Again, in "Sim Mode" you won't be able 
         to place an attack any greater than fifteen miles from one of 
         your brigades.
         
         The new kind of attack occurs when you set an enemy site as the 
         explicit destination of one of your brigades. This is the only 
         way to capture a site directly and strategically with StratFalc 
         (you can occupy a site NONstrategically by simply using the 
         Strat/Occupy submode). This new method of attack is called, 
         logically enough, a "Capture Attack."
         
         To accomplish a Capture Attack, you use only the Strat/Sim 
         submode's "Grnd" screen. Instead of moving one of your brigades 
         *near* an enemy site, you move it right *on to* a site. A dialog 
         box will confirm that you want to attempt to capture the site. 
         Unlike an Attrition Attack, in a Capture Attack you don't have 
         to activate any sites inside Falcon 3.0: the battle is fought 
         totally inside Strategic Falcon's little brain.
         
         When you commit to this type of attack, StratFalc examines the 
         situation and calculates the outcome of the battle. You will 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 13
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         discover this outcome after you fly the current mission and come 
         back to StratFalc. If you are victorious, your brigade may take 
         some casualties but you will occupy the site. If you lose, YOUR 
         BRIGADE WILL BE WIPED OUT!
        
         Did that get your attention? Good. You probably would like to 
         know a little more about how StratFalc decides the outcome of 
         the battle, so you can maximize your chances for success. 
         
         The first and foremost consideration is (you guessed it!) 
         how well you do in your current Falcon mission. (This all 
         depends on you!) If you accomplish your mission, the brigade 
         will fight *much* more effectively.
         
         The second most important consideration is the quantity 
         and type of units involved. If you've got a brigade 
         with 40 tanks going up against a bridge that only has 
         10 BMPs for defense, you can rest assured you're going 
         to mop it up with little trouble. However, if that same 
         brigade goes up against a site garrisoned with 60 tanks 
         and artillery, the outcome is doubtful.
         
         The third consideration is the energy level of your brigade 
         (which will be discussed in the next section). It pays to rest 
         your troops before sending them into battle. Generally, any 
         troops whose energy level is below 10 will suffer degradation of 
         battle effectiveness. However, the adverse effect doesn't really 
         become pronounced until the energy level hits 5 or 6: after 
         that, their ability to fight falls off sharply--sending in 
         troops with energy levels of 1 or 2 is almost certain suicide!
         
         The final factor in deciding these battles is whether the site 
         in question is a victory node for either side. The enemy will 
         fight harder to hold onto one of its victory sites or to keep 
         you from capturing one of yours.
         
         So, taking these considerations into account, you might discern 
         a prudent manner of attack against an important site: whittle 
         it down before committing to the final offensive! Move your 
         brigade to within 15 miles of the site but don't attempt to 
         occupy it just yet. While the brigade rests up for the 
         Capture Attack, send a few Attrition Attacks against the site 
         to weaken it. Only when you have an overwhelming numerical 
         advantage should you finally order your brigade to move in. 
         (Of course, it's up to you--you may not be this type of 
         commander!)
         
         -Energy and Fatigue-
         
         Energy and Fatigue are the same thing looked at from opposite 
         sides. At the beginning of a campaign, your brigades are fully 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 14
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         rested and have a perfect Energy level of 15. As they carry out 
         operations in the theater, they burn up this energy.
         
         You can tell any of your brigades' current energy level from the 
         Strat/Sim Grnd screen. If this number gets to zero, the brigade 
         can't move or carry out any form of attack. If you manage to get 
         this number below 0 (which is entirely possible!), you'll wish 
         you hadn't!
         
         Everything costs energy, from moving along the back roads to 
         carrying out attacks against the enemy. But you gain energy as 
         well. Every six hours, you regain 2 energy points--back up to 
         the maximum of 15. If you leave the brigade in an allied city or 
         at an allied base, it gains 3 points. But if a brigade is 
         trapped behind enemy lines, it will only gets back only 1 
         point in those same six hours.
         
         Any "Attrition Attack" costs one point of energy for the entire
         brigade. The cost of a "Capturing Attack" varies from one to 
         three points, depending on the status of the adversary. 
         
         -Brigade Movement-
         
         The movement rate of each brigade depends on several factors. 
         These include the terrain it travels over (roads or non-roads), 
         the speed you set for it, and its energy level.
         
         A fully rested brigade moving along roads, at normal speed, and 
         encountering no resistance will cover 40 miles in six hours. (We 
         have to make allowances for logistics and constant mechanical 
         breakdowns.) The same brigade moving at doubletime speed will 
         move 60 miles, and at maximum speed it will cover 80 miles.
         
         If the brigade is off the roads, cut each of the above distances 
         in half.
         
         In addition, movement tires your units and costs them energy 
         points. Normal travel along roads uses energy at a level of 3 
         points every six hours.
         
         However, as mentioned above, every six hours your units regain 2 
         points of energy. So, while moving at normal speed over roads,
         they lose a net energy of only 1 point. At this rate, they could 
         travel for more than three days before they collapse from 
         exhaustion.
         
         Things change when you press them too hard. Energy costs go up a 
         point for each increase in speed rate, and moving off-road 
         costs another point. If you put your units at top speed 
         off-road, they'll burn up 6 units of energy. Since they only 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 15
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         recoup 2, they'll lose a net of 4. They'll start dropping like 
         flies soon after one day of this treatment!
         
         The tables below summarize movement, its rates, and associated 
         energy costs.
         
         -Energy Cost-
                                 Points
                        Normal Dbl-Time Maximum
         On-Road           3       4       5 
         Off-Road          4       5       6
         
         -Energy Recuperation-
                             Points Regained 
                    Normal   In City/Base   Enemy Terr 
                      2            3             1
         
         -Movement Rates-
         
         Beginning energy level 11 to 15: 
                              Distance 
                      Normal Dbl-Time Maximum 
         On-Road        40      60      80 
         Off-Road       20      30      40
         
         Beginning energy level 6 to 10: 
                              Distance 
                      Normal Dbl-Time Maximum 
         On-Road        30      50      70 
         Off-Road       15      25      35
         
                             
         Beginning energy level 1 to 5: 
                              Distance
                       Normal Dbl-Time Maximum 
         On-Road         20      40      60 
         Off-Road        10      20      30 
                             
         Do you see the trouble? Strategic Falcon doesn't tell you how to 
         command your troops--it only tells you what happens when you 
         issue certain commands. There is nothing to stop you from, say, 
         ordering a brigade whose current energy level is 2 to move at 
         maximum speed off-road. 
         
         As you can see from the first table, doing so will cost you 6 
         energy points! You'll end up with your troops in a negative 
         energy state--which means they're beyond exhaustion. Disaster 
         will occur! If you ever do anything that would leave you below 0 
         in energy, your brigades will start self-destructing: traffic 
         accidents will flare up, friendly fire massacres will break out, 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 16
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         and people will, in general, be very testy. As mentioned in the 
         "Fatigue" section, if a brigade's energy state is at or below 
         zero, you won't be able to issue it any commands, so this
         danger occurs when it is very close to, but above, zero.
      
         -Roads-
         
         The "roads" in Falcon are the links between the sites. If you 
         assign a brigade to move forward, it will try to do so by moving 
         along these roads. To stay on the road, you must figure a 
         site-to-site path to your eventual destination, and put the 
         brigade's destination marker directly on the either the next 
         friendly site in that path or somewhere on the gray line leading 
         to it. 
         
         If StratFalc decides that you're ordering a brigade to leave the 
         road, it will ask you to confirm this. If you keep getting a 
         confirmation dialog box but you're placing a destination marker 
         on the gray line, first check that the site you're aiming for is 
         indeed a neighbor of the site you're leaving. Bear in mind 
         that some roads are "one-way." You can see these roads in the 
         "Lines" submode--if you highlight the site which holds your 
         brigade, the bright yellow lines show all the possible 
         "on-road" destinations.
         
         An important note: Once you leave the road, the ONLY way to
         get back on it is to move the brigade to a site (any site).
         
         -The Front Line-
         
         Every six hours your brigades stop and do a complete 
         reconnaissance. They look in all directions. If one is 
         surrounded on three sides by the enemy, it will move forward, 
         but will do so very slowly. It may also begin to take 
         casualties because it is making an opposed move. (These 
         casualties are not as drastic as they would be if it runs into 
         an enemy brigade, however!)
         
         Very often you must advance into enemy territory to conquer 
         sites. Try to minimize the number of turns you spend back 
         there. Beside being sniped at by the enemy, yoou brigade will 
         also regain strength at the rate of only 1 point per turn. 
         
         -Water-
         
         Water was problematic in writing the movement code. I decided 
         either I'd have to constantly compare the brigades' locations to 
         those of the Falcon shorelines, and so add in specific (and 
         extremely slow) code for each theater, or simplify matters and 
         speed things up. I chose to simplify matters, so here's how it 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 17
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         works:
         
         When a brigade does its reconnaissance, it looks north, east, 
         south, and west of its present location. If it doesn't see any 
         sites at all in a particular direction, it won't go that way, 
         period. This method has its drawbacks. In Bosnia, for instance, 
         the airbase at Brindisi, Italy, in the southwest corner of the 
         theater, is across the Adriatic from Bosnia. However, because of 
         these movement rules, if you have a brigade on the coast of 
         Bosnia and order it to go west, into the sea, it will see no 
         problem with that and will happily oblige--like a bunch of 
         lemmings! It does this because it sees those bases to the west 
         and says "okay." Maybe they found some transports somewhere.
         
         On the other hand, these same movement rules allow you to move
         your brigades onto the rocky beaches of the Kurile Islands.
         
         The point is, it's up to you to order your brigades to move 
         sensibly.
      
         -Falcon CAS Missions-
         
         Units which are placed by Falcon 3.0 in support of the current 
         mission come out of your Division. They will be taken from the 
         nearest brigade unless that brigade doesn't have enough 
         units. If StratFalc has raided all three of your brigades and 
         still doesn't have enough units to satisfy Falcon's placement 
         (a *very* rare occurrence!) it will take units from your 
         sites to make up the difference.
         
         The fifteen mile rule is overridden in this case; it's assumed 
         one or two of your companies got lost somehow and ended up 
         in the mission's target area (fog of war and all that).
         
         This puts a whole new slant on Falcon itself. Successfully 
         completing a CAS mission is now *extremely* important. If you 
         fail, your brigades will lose most--and maybe all--of the 
         allied units that are involved in the mission. 
         
         For this reason, you should feel free to tweak those missions 
         where Falcon places two opposing columns too close to each other 
         and by so doing makes the mission impossible to accomplish (see 
         "2-columnn CAS missions" under "Impossible Missions" in your 
         StratFalc manual).
      
                                  
                                  LOADOUTS
      
         Another major new feature in StratFalc version 1.1 is the 
         addition of "Loadouts" to the Aircraft Arming Dialog. With 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 18
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         these controls, you can automatically save and load the 
         appropriate weapons to any aircraft.
          
         Each aircraft type has its own (individual) set of 18 loadouts. 
         Default loadouts for each plane are provided, but these are 
         only roughed in and can be adjusted or renamed to suit your 
         needs.
      
         Using Loadouts:
      
         The first thing you'll probably notice in the Arming Dialog 
         box (you get to this box by pressing the "Arms" button in any 
         Tactical/Air submode) is the red section at the bottom of the 
         box. This contains a very brief description of the aircraft's 
         normal loads and available hard points. If you wish to change 
         the notes in this box, use a text editor to alter the 
         appropriate "*.NFO" file (e.g., the one for A-10s is 
         A-10.NFO). Here are the rules: the file size cannot be 
         greater than 350 bytes when you're finished and StratFalc 
         will ignore carriage return/linefeeds. (Thanks to Miles 
         Constable for supplying the appropriate notes!)
         
         To use a loadout, simply click on the "Use..." button in the 
         Arming Dialog box. A new dialog box will pop up. You'll see 
         18 radio buttons, each labeled with either some cryptic 
         designation or the word "Unused." An explanation of the 
         labels is given below. 
         
         To examine the contents of a loadout, just click on the 
         appropriate radio button. The bottom of the dialog will fill 
         with a description of that particular loadout. To load the 
         weapons you're viewing, click the "use" button. The dialog 
         will close and the Arming Dialog will now contain the desired 
         weapons. Adjust the weapon quantities if desired, then click 
         on "OK" to finalize loading the aircraft.
         
         One thing should be mentioned. To use these loadouts effectively 
         (indeed, to load any aircraft effectively with StratFalc), you 
         should be comfortable with the alphanumeric designation of the 
         various ordnance. For instance, you should know off the top of 
         your head that an AGM-88A is a HARM (High-speed Anti-Radiation 
         Missile). If you don't, please do some studying of the reference 
         section of the Falcon manual; it will be time well spent.
         
         The default labels follow these conventions (up to a point):
         
         The first 3 or 4 letters tell you the suitable mission types for 
         the weaponry.               
         
            ATA      Air to Air 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 19
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

            CAS      Combat Air Strike 
            BOMB     Stationary Target Strike 
            SEAD     Suppression of Enemy Air Defense
      
         After the hyphen come any amplification of weapon types.
         
         For example, for ATA loadouts:       
             BVR Exclusively beyond visual range missiles (AMRAAMS) 
             WVR Exclusively within visual range missiles (Sidewinders)
         
         For CAS or Bomb:
             PGM Precision Guided Munitions 
             UG Unguided Munitions
         
         (If there is no designator the loadout is a mix.)
         
         An extra letter after the slash signifies the size of the 
         individual munitions. "/H" is Heavy; "/L" is Light; "/M" is 
         Medium. Again, no slash letter indicates a mix.
         
         Finally, the last section designates the aircraft's relative 
         range and hence, is a rough indicator of how much firepower it's 
         carrying.
         
         LR     Long Range--some weapons will be replaced by fuel tanks 
         SR     Short Range--no fuel tanks and extra weapons
         
         (If there is no designator, there is no extra fuel. Neither are 
         there any extra weapons.)
         
         As an example, a loadout of ATA-BVR-SR on an F-15 would signify 
         a jet that is loaded down with 8 AMRAAMs. ATA-BVR-LR might mean 
         6 AMRAAMs and external fuel.
         
         On a B-52, a loadout of BOMB-UG/H would mean 24 Mk-84 (2000 lb.) 
         iron bombs.
         
         
         Saving Loadouts:
         
         To save a loadout, first you must have the appropriate aircraft 
         type as the active one. Then click on the "Arms" button. Then 
         you simply fill in the Arming Dialog with the weapon loadout you 
         want to save and click the "Save..." button. A new dialog will 
         appear and will ask you the slot in which you want to save this 
         loadout. (The first slot labeled "Unused," if there is one, will 
         be preselected for you. Otherwise the first slot will be 
         preselected.) Select the slot you prefer and click "OK."
         
         If the slot you choose is already named, you will get one more 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 20
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         chance to abort the process; otherwise you'll be prompted for 
         the name of the new loadout.
         
         
         Editing Loadouts:
         
         To change a loadout, first you need to fill the Arming Dialog 
         box with the weapons you wish to place in the loadout. The 
         fastest way to do this is to bring up the dialog box, press 
         "Use...", and then select the loadout that you want to alter. 
         Click "OK" to fill the boxes in the Arming Dialog. Alter the 
         loadout however you want, and then click "Save...". Select the 
         same loadout name as the one you were altering, and click "OK." 
         
         
         More information on Loadouts:
         
         The reason for the addition of the Loadout features is the 
         difficulty in arming aircraft and the ease with which they 
         can be overloaded. Each default loadout has been tested to 
         make sure the aircraft will fly properly. This saves you the 
         aggravation of precision-planning a mission only to see your 
         pilots do a series of low speed stalls into a hillside (and 
         why don't they just dump their stores, anyway?).
         
         Also, feel free to share your loadout files with others.  
         You'll notice that I've supplied only 3 or 4 loadouts for 
         most aircraft. That's because building these is a very 
         time-consuming operation. Each loadout data file has the 
         extension LOD (for example, the F-14 loadout is "F-14.LOD"). 
         If you build up some nice sets, put them up on CIS or any 
         other board. ONLY THE LOADOUT FILES, of course, none of the 
         money stuff (not the EXEs)!
      
         There is more to creating a successful loadout than arming 
         the aircraft and keeping the weight below that in the "Max" 
         box.  Drag affects loadouts too.  You might be able to place 
         20,000 lbs of Mk-84 bombs on a plane, but 20,000 lbs of 
         Sidewinders would cause trouble. For this reason, its a good 
         idea to test any new loadouts that may be large enough to 
         cause problems.
      
         A special note about BUFFs:
      
         Heavier aircraft in general and B-52s in particular are very 
         easy to crash. A good rule of thumb is to never assign a 
         wingman in the first F-16 flight to a B-52, because no matter 
         what you do, they start too close to the ground. If you must 
         put one in your own flight, make him "extra light" and get 
         off the ground as soon as you can. That first racetrack turn 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 21
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         while he's waiting for you to get airborne is a killer!
      
         Also, be *very* careful with waypoint altitudes. A B-52 is
         not going to climb too steeply, but it'll try like hell to 
         get where you tell it to go. If you place waypoint 2 at 9000 
         feet and only two miles from waypoint 1 at 2000 feet, your 
         B-52s will actually end up at waypoint 1.5 at 0 feet (and in 
         a twisted mass of metal).
                                
         
                                SPEED LIMITS
      
         Speed limits will now be enforced! If you change an aircraft 
         type in a flight, and the speed of any of that flight's 
         waypoints are greater than the maximum speed of that aircraft, 
         then the offending waypoint(s) speed will be lowered to the max 
         speed of the aircraft. In other words, the flight won't be able 
         to go faster than the slowest flier. If you don't like this, you 
         can disable the feature with the "/NOSPEEDCHECK" switch. 
      
         This can cause a problem if you add a slow aircraft and 
         subsequently remove it: the waypoints will all be "too slow." To 
         alleviate this problem a bit, I've made it easier to enter the 
         value "450" (which seems to be the most common waypoint speed) 
         in the speed box. If you click on the speed box, then hit the 
         <n> key ("n" for "normal"), then hit <Enter>, "450" will 
         automatically take the place of that "n" in the speed box.
         
         The smart thing to do to avoid frustration is assign all 
         aircraft to a flight *first*, before you start tweaking 
         waypoints.
         
         Also, because of some limitations with storage in the data 
         files, the fastest speed for any aircraft in Falcon3 is 1275 
         knots. 
      
         Please note that the maximum speeds are taken from various 
         sources (Falcon manuals, Jane's, etc.) and that the actual top 
         speeds of aircraft may be less in the simulation, depending on 
         weapon loads, altitude, etc.  For this reason, the "Timing Trace"
         feature may mislead you:  you can still *set* a speed higher than 
         the aircraft may actually fly, although you can't set it higher 
         than the aircraft's *absolute* limit.
                                
      
                                FUEL REPLACEMENT
      
         As you may have noticed by now, Falcon 3.0 robs your
         fuel.  If you exit from the cockpit in Falcon by using the 
         "ALT-X" method or by pulling down the menu and hitting 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 22
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         "Quit to DOS," the fuel currently loaded in your F-16s does 
         not return to your inventory.  To make Falcon put it back in 
         the storage tanks, you have to use "Abort Mission" and then 
         exit to DOS from the mission planning screens.
      
         However, with StratFalc 1.1, this problem is just about solved.
         First, though, YOU MUST HAVE SIM MODE ACTIVE for this feature 
         to work (otherwise StratFalc has no way of tracking your fuel 
         mission to mission).  If StratFalc sees your fuel inventory
         drop suspiciously, it'll boost it back up to its previous 
         level.  It knows real use, though, so you still have to 
         manage your fuel--this isn't a panacea!
      
      
                              ONE KEY TO FLIGHT
      
         If you hit the <F4> key, StratFalc will exit and start Falcon, 
         and when you exit, you'll be back in StratFalc again (this 
         even works while in the KCC menu program!). This feature 
         works through batch file processing, so it uses none of the 
         system's memory. Okay, virtually none.
      
                                                    
                        CHOOSE YOUR FLIGHT WITH EASE
      
         You can now instantly switch your first Falcon flight with any 
         other flight. Just hit <F3>.
                        
         This feature will let you swap F-16 flight 1 automatically with 
         any other F-16 flight. If, for example, you draw a CAS missions 
         and you want fly CAP instead of hitting the column, you don't 
         have to painstakingly swap all Flight 2's waypoints for flight
         1's--you just swap the flights. 
      
                            
                           MORE RELIABLE AIRCRAFT
                                
         There's a new limiter on StratFalc flights to better reflect 
         what Falcon actually places in the theater--total aircraft is 
         now 14, not 16 as stated in the manual.  It turns out that 
         Falcon almost always keeps two aircraft in reserve in 
         campaigns (those randoms gotta come from somewhere!). 
      
      
