                                  Sim Mode:
      
      Q. The documentation states that number of forces is calculated 
         depending on the configuration campaign setting, i.e. "easy 
         etc.". What happens if you change that setting in the middle 
         of a campaign?
      
      A. Nothing. Your forces are set at the beginning and that's what 
         they'll stay. However, changing the difficulty in midstream 
         *will* change the formula used to determine wins/losses. For 
         example, if you go to easier, less of your guys will get blown 
         away.
      
      Q. While it may be more realistic to start the allied flights
         from their respective bases, it's also a major pain. Realism 
         is great, but we don't have any control to launch the allied 
         flights early to rendezvous. For example: CAS mission in 
         Pakistan. I got to move the mud, but my escort was 75 nmi 
         away, while my target was 17 nmi from my own base. Lost 3 
         Vipers on that one.
      
      A. Well, that's where the management aspect comes in! Your 
         *default* escort is 75 miles away--but you're the commander now 
         and you can override this obvious error of an underling!
      
         I think the thing to do is examine what squadrons are stationed 
         nearby (or at your base), and then change the aircraft type of 
         the escort flight to an appropriate, nearby aircraft type. 
         StratFalc will offer to move the first waypoint to the proper 
         base for you.
         
         Meanwhile, see if it's worthwhile to transfer that distant 
         squadron somewhere closer. StratFalc initially places all 
         aircraft at bases depending on (1) the type of aircraft and (2) 
         the bases' distance from the front (for example, E-3s always 
         start out pretty far from the action). What I've been doing 
         personally is, at the outset of a campaign, juggling my 
         squadrons. Most of the time I just ignore Falcon's "suggestion" 
         of a support flight and change it to something I think is more 
         appropriate for logistic or strategic reasons.
         
         Another option is to delay your F-16s (fly around and wait for a 
         while?), until the help gets there. But, of course, then the 
         enemy randoms will show up like FOD on a hangar deck!
      
      Q. F3 doesn't recognize StratFalc site captures in its Theater 
         Updates even though the icons show up blue in F3. Is there any 
         way that the changes could be written into the code to modify 
         the file to reflect the actual changes?


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 24
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

      
      A. In a word, no. Falcon has it's own routine going on and I 
         don't want to mess with it. (It took a while to decide to 
         change site-capture at all!) The "capture" announcement that 
         StratFalc gives you was the best I could come up with. 
      
      Q. Am I correct in assuming that F3 *does* recognize the sites 
         you've captured with SF, but doesn't add them to the allied 
         gains list?
      
      A. Oh sure, Falcon recognizes them. When you fly over the sites, 
         there are friendly units down there (with the exception of 
         "transcendentals" discussed in a later question), and when F3 
         does its victory checks, they count as yours (which is really 
         all that counts!). They just don't get added to the 0600 
         "captured" list because Falcon didn't capture them using it's 
         own routine. Falcon thinks the sites were either captured 
         earlier in the campaign or they were always yours.
         
         In a related vein, one of my fears is that StratFalc's sim mode 
         makes it just too easy to win a campaign. Making the brigades 
         work in the manner they're intended is a fine balance, and I 
         honestly don't know if I've got it right. I know one thing: if 
         one gets ambushed behind enemy lines, it's hell to extract it!
      
      Q. Is it true that you can issue an attack order for a site 
         that you already call your own?
      
      A. Yep, you can issue the order. Won't do you any good, of 
         course, but there's nothing to stop you! It could be changed, 
         I suppose, but why bother? (For some perverse reason, I like 
         it!)
      
      Q. What happens to the StratFalc planes that were shot down 
         during the mission? Are they lost, or not?
      
      A. The short answer is: they might be! 
      
         The long answer is:
         
         StratFalc has no way to see *directly* what happened during the 
         mission to any planes other than F-16s (and even then, it 
         doesn't know if F-16s were shot down or another type that was in 
         an F-16 slot). What it does is see how well (or poorly) the 
         mission went and the extrapolate that for any StratFalc planes 
         (or ground units) that were placed "At Risk" before the mission 
         began. 
         
         Say you have 4 F-16s flying, with 2 F-15s in StratFalc (Allied) 
         slots for support. In this case, the F-15s are placed "At Risk." 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 25
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         You fly the mission and lose two F-16s and the two F-15s *in 
         Falcon*, but still succeed in accomplishing the mission.
         
         Then you return to StratFalc, which sees (1) that you succeeded 
         in your mission but (2) that you lost 2 F-16s in the process 
         (again, it doesn't see that the F-15s were lost). It checks the 
         difficulty, looks to see how many enemy planes you shot down, 
         looks to see how many ground targets you destroyed, etc., and 
         comes up with a percentage--a probability--that all units that 
         were placed "at risk" survived the mission. Then it "rolls" 
         against the units at risk using that percentage. If the unit is 
         destroyed, it tells you so.
         
         So you can see, then, what you lose in Falcon for the StratFalc 
         planes doesn't matter. Therefore...
      
      Q. Does Stratfalc incur extra losses over what was lost in the 
         mission if the mission is not successful?
      
      A. No, not extra. You lose F-16s in Falcon. You lose the 
         StratFalc (or Allied) planes in StratFalc. And...
      
      Q. Is StratFalc rewriting the mission history based solely on my 
         success or failure?
      
      A. Yes, but again, only regarding the StratFalc (Allied) planes. 
         Whatever happened to the aircraft in your F-16 slots in the 
         mission remains as it happened.
      
         Just to make this explicit: only the StratFalc (Allied) flights 
         are placed "At Risk." F-16 slots are left alone, no matter what 
         aircraft type you put in them. This is to prevent "double 
         jeopardy." If F-16 slot aircraft were put in the "At Risk" pool, 
         as well as the StratFalc planes, then you'd face losing them 
         both as available F-16s in Falcon and in StratFalc.
         
         Also, this offers an additional indirect method of resupplying 
         StratFalc aircraft. As one user put it:
      
                             ----------------- 
             In a real campaign, would F16s be the only A/C that are 
