      Q. I've conquered a site but when I fly near it, I still get locked 
         up by enemy SAMs, with a TWI code of 5. The site shows up as 
         blue in both StratFalc and Falcon. What gives? Do I own the 
         site or not?
      
      A. Congratulations! You've run into a "transcendental." You own 
         the site, all right, but there are some weird visitors 
         sharing it with your units. "Transcendental" units are the 
         fixed SAM emplacements. On the Allied side, they're Patriot 
         missile batteries. On the Enemy side, they're the SA-2 
         missile sites.
         
         I call them "transcendentals" because they're active ground 
         units, associated with a site, that transcend Falcon's normal
         Kill Box activation scheme. They're always active and they're 
         not limited to one kill box (they're also the only ground 
         units active in Red Flag). They don't exist in the original 
         three theaters (Kuwait, Israel and Panama), only in Pakistan, 
         Japan, Korea and Bosnia. Technically, they're *active* map 
         objects, not site objects. (The ships in Japan are also 
         transcendentals.) 
         
         They live by some very strange rules. Enemy ground units
         don't "see" them, so when a transcendental is at a site, and 
         that site is captured by the other side, the transcendental 
         may peacefully coexist with the site's new tenants. It will 
         still lock up and fire on aircraft. It's like they exist in a 
         separate dimension than the other ground units, which is 
         another reason to call them transcendental. (Actually, the 
         ground units are treating them just as if they were any other 
         structure at the site.)
         
         However, while ground units can't "see" them, your aircraft 
         surely can, and a SEAD flight will knock them out once and 
         for all, for the remainder of the campaign.
         
         How do you deal with them? Well, since the ground units won't 
         do the dirty work of destroying them, you'll have to. If, 
         after your side captures a site, you're still getting locked 
         up (and launched at) from that site (as your question 
         indicates you are), then you've got to take action to knock 
         out the transcendentals yourself. The sites are, namely, the 
         fixed "missile sites." Their icon in Falcon shows a missile.
         
         There is one nice part to all this: When performing a strike 
         on transcendentals, you don't have to wake up the site, so 
         AGM-88s are perfect for this type of attack--they're long 
         range/standoff, and in this instance there's no artificial 
         restriction on their use caused by Kill Box boundaries. Not 
         only that, but you don't have to go with the strike force or


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 29
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         view it--you can even task a StratFalc/Allied flight to do 
         the job while you're using your F-16 flights to perform the 
         current mission. You then have no risk of losing your own 
         aircraft (cowardly as that sounds!).
         
         Since these units exist no matter who occupies the site, you 
         can attack them at any time. SA-2 sites are easily spotted, 
         but if the enemy still owns the site, a SEAD flight may spend 
         its ordnance on other SAMs without destroying the 
         transcendentals. For this reason, you may want to avoid 
         attacking these units until you capture the site, when your 
         strike force will have no problem picking them out (they'll 
         be the only enemy units to hit!).
         
         If you're set on destroying them before you capture the 
         site, though, there is a way. Since they transcend Kill Box 
         rules, just approach the site from the closest kill box 
         border, but don't cross the border. The transcendentals will 
         light up (SA-2s will track you from 45 miles away or more!) 
         while the other units at the site will remain asleep. Let 
         loose a HARM or two at the offending SAMs.
         
         And a final note about transcendentals. As map objects,
         they're immune to the "Zoom" level you set in Falcon's 
         "Options" menu. They will always be 1X size, which may make 
         attacking them even harder.
      
                              Troubleshooting:
      
      Q. Sometimes when I'm changing something with the mouse, I 
         have to keep clicking it to make it change.
       
      A. You have to wait for SF to finish redrawing the screen before 
         you hit another control. The code doesn't get back to polling 
         the mouse until the screen is in place, because otherwise it 
         wouldn't know which controls were "in focus." 
      
      Q. I want to be sure that I understand Primary Warning Number 5 
         ("THOU SHALT FORCE FALCON 3.0 TO CREATE A NEW *.MIS FILE FOR 
         EACH NEW MISSION.") completely. My understanding after 
         reading the manual would be that you would enter Falcon 3, 
         look at the mission and determine what changes to make, then 
         start StratFalc, make the changes, and save them. Here is 
         where I want to be sure that I have it correct, now when I 
         start Falcon 3 after I have made the StratFalc changes, DO I 
         EXIT THE PROGRAM AFTER HITTING THE "TAKEOFF" BUTTON AT THIS 
         TIME, AND THEN RESTART THE GAME AND THE MISSION? In other 
         words enter the program, leave and then enter the program 
         again?
      


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 30
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

      A. OK, warning 5 means that you have to get into the cockpit (or 
         more correctly, press the "Takeoff" button) at least once for 
         each mission before entering StratFalc. This is because 
         StratFalc needs access to the "squad?.mis" file which is only 
         created when you press the "Takeoff" button. A typical session 
         would go like this:
         
         1.) Start Falcon, and either start or continue a campaign. Go 
             through all the screens--look at the mission description, 
             but don't bother memorizing it. It's always available in 
             StratFalc if you hit <F2>. 
      
         2.) Continue through the Falcon screens and press the "Takeoff" 
             button. Once you're in the cockpit, exit to DOS.
      
         3.) Start Strategic Falcon. Open the campaign you were using in 
             Falcon. Adjust all flights, F-16 and otherwise, and 
             everything else the way you want it.
      
         4.) Start Falcon again and go through into the cockpit. You 
             don't need to pause in any mission screens--you've done all 
             this in StratFalc. All you have to do is choose the pilots 
             for the aircraft in your F-16 slots. Fly the mission.
      
         5.) At the end of the mission, go through the debrief. Press 
             "Next Mission," and hurry through to the cockpit again. 
             Return to step #2 above.
         
         So you can see, you only exit Falcon *once* between missions. 
         When you're done fighting for the night, just make sure you 
         leave Falcon from the cockpit (and so create a new 
         squad*.mis), and for your next session, start by modifying 
         that mission with StratFalc.
      
      
