         STRTSPRT TSR loaded when playing Falcon3.  The number two cause is
         abandoning a campaign before its conclusion.  If you ever abandon
         a campaign, run "CLEAR" before starting a new one.
      
      
      
      
               And finally, here's a tip from your Uncle Bill.       
      
      
            Attacking Enemy Sites:  How to Live to Tell About It.  
      
      
         A successful strike against a well-defended site is more 
         than just placing a waypoint and sending in some heavily 
         loaded F-111s. Doing this will destroy some enemy units, but 
         it will also, inevitably, lose the bombers. That is not a 
         successful strike!
         
         The first thing you have to do to pull off this type of 
         attack is "honor the threat." For our purposes, we'll assume 
         that the site we're going to destroy is a full-capacity, 
         full-strength site. In Falcon 3.0, that means 60 units--more 
         than half of which are anti-air of some form. Even though no 
         more than about 35 will appear at one time, this is more than 
         4 F-111s can handle; it is probably more than any amount of 
         aircraft you have to spare can handle in one attack.
         
         So here's the first rule:
         
         * Don't attempt to destroy all units at a full-strength site 
           with a single attack. 
           
         "Well, great," you say, "then how do I tell my fully-armed 
         bombers to break off? There's no way to tell them to hit only 
         a few units each and then go home."
         
         Yes there is. To make the aircraft only commit to a limited 
         attack, you have to do two things: make their attack waypoint 
         easy to achieve and limit the load of the lead plane.
         
         It helps if you try to "think" like a computer pilot (who is 
         really just a robot following your orders). He is going to do 
         what you tell him to do, or he's going to die trying. When 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 34
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         you set a waypoint, you are telling his little silicon brain 
         to fly to that waypoint--this much is obvious. However, if 
         that waypoint is in the center of the site in question, he 
         has to fly over quite a few nasty items to get to it.
         
         This isn't exactly what you have in mind--flying *over* the 
         nasty items--what you want him to do is *destroy* those 
         items. But he's flying fast; he can only destroy so many bad 
         guys before he starts flying over the rest of them.
         
         And as he flies over the ones he cannot destroy, they start 
         locking him up with radar and then start shooting at him. 
         Avoiding an incoming missile has priority in his little brain 
         (we said he was a robot--we didn't say he was stupid!), so he 
         dodges the missile and in so doing he turns away from the 
         waypoint. After he's defeated that threat, he goes for the 
         waypoint again--only to be locked up once more and shot at. 
         Sooner or later, if he keeps dancing around up there, he's 
         going to get dead. And by this time, he's too busy dodging 
         missiles to pull off any meaningful attacks. He should have 
         called it quits by now.
         
         That's why you should make the waypoint easy to achieve. Once 
         he has flown to the point in space where you told him to fly, 
         he's free to go to the next waypoint. Exit, stage left. 
         
         In Falcon, ground units are generally distributed to the 
         northwest of the site to which they're assigned. A site with 
         sixty units will have those units scattered throughout an 
         area of about seven square miles, with the site itself 
         (normally) in the southeast corner, about a mile inside the 
         perimeter. The attack waypoint should be placed *just 
         outside* the perimeter--not in the center of it!--on the same 
         side as the direction from which the aircraft are attacking.
         
         For example, if your aircraft are coming in from the 
         northwest, the attack waypoint should be placed about 8.5 
         miles northwest (6 miles north, 6 miles west) of the site; if 
         they're coming in from the southeast, the waypoint would be 
         about 1.5 miles southeast of the site.
         
         As the planes come in, they'll start shooting. Once they 
         reach their waypoint, don't worry, they'll keep shooting--but 
         now they're free to leave once they've used up their weapons 
         or if things get too tough.
                                                                      
         The second thing you have to do to keep an attack under 
         control is limit the quantity of weapons on the lead plane. 
         This is really to safeguard the wingmen--they don't fly to 
         waypoints; they fly to a specific point in space that's 


                                                                          


      Strategic FALCON            Version 1.1 Update               Page 35
      --------------------------------------------------------------------

         relative to the leader. (If the leader is destroyed, the 
         first wingman takes his place.) If the leader is still 
         circling over the site trying to shoot targets after his 
         wingmen have gone winchester, then the wingmen are taking a 
         large and nonproductive risk (it's one thing to risk getting 
         shot down while you're actively destroying the enemy--it's 
         quite another to risk getting shot down just because your 
         programming tells you to stay on the wing of your leader!). 
         It behooves you to try to make the lead plane run out of 
         ordnance first.
         
         Don't worry that the wingmen will bug out immediately once 
         the leader goes--they'll stay and take good shots while they 
         can, but just as you want the leader to be free to leave, you 
         want the wingmen to be free to leave.
         
         Using these pointers, you can increase the survivability of 
         your planes and hence increase your chances of winning the 
         campaign.
